<comment datatype='string'>AtoB is one of two 3-ketoacyl-CoA thiolases in &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt;.  While &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=FADA-MONOMER&quot;&gt;FadA&lt;/a&gt; has broad substrate specificity, AtoB is strictly specific for acetoacetyl-CoA [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/236278&quot;&gt;Feigenbaum75&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/9904&quot;&gt;Duncombe76&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
Crystal structures of AtoB alone and in a complex with CoA have been solved; the structure can be described as a loosely interacting dimer of tight dimers [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/27380370&quot;&gt;Ithayaraja16&lt;/a&gt;].  The gene for this study was cloned from &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt; BL21 (DE3), but the sequence is identical to that of the K-12 protein.
&lt;p&gt;
AtoB expression is induced by growth on acetoacetate [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/4563344&quot;&gt;Pauli72&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
AtoB is used for metabolic engineering of 1-butanol [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/21398484&quot;&gt;Shen11&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/25866563&quot;&gt;Menon15&lt;/a&gt;] and medium-chain fuel [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/25645093&quot;&gt;Kim15d&lt;/a&gt;] production.
&lt;p&gt;
AtoB: &quot;&lt;b&gt;a&lt;/b&gt;ce&lt;b&gt;to&lt;/b&gt;acetate&quot; [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/4563344&quot;&gt;Pauli72&lt;/a&gt;]</comment>
